  "query_preview": "(This evening through Sunday)...\nIssued at 156 PM EST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nSurface analysis this afternoon shows strong high pressure in place \nover IL. Cool NW surface flow was in place over Indiana. GOES19 \nshows Lake cloud streaming across central Indiana. These clouds were \nnarrow, impacting Kokomo, Indianapolis, Columbus and Bloomington \nwhile points to the west were under sunshine. Additional diurnal \nstratocu had developed over eastern Central Indiana.  Aloft, water \nvapor imagery shows strong ridging in place over the plains and \nupper midwest extending into western Ontario. This was resulting in \nlee side subsidence across Indiana within the column. Temperatures \nwere in the 40s.\n\nTonight...\n\nThe combination of weak warm air advection arriving from the west \nalong with the loss of diurnal heating should allow for skies across \nCentral Indiana to clear this evening and overnight. The surface \nhigh pressure system to the west is expected to build eastward to \nIndiana and Ohio overnight. Forecast soundings and time heights also \ntrend toward a dry column. Low dew point depressions along with \nclear skies and light winds may result in patchy diurnal fog \ndevelopment overnight. Look for lows in the lower 30s.\n\nMonday through Tuesday...\n\nThe strong ridging aloft over the upper midwest is expected to sag \nsoutheast, building across Central Indiana on Tuesday and Wednesday. \nThis results in strong mid level subsidence through the start of the \nworkweek. Furthermore, by Tuesday the ridging to the west rebuilds \nand strengthens, keeping additional subsidence and drying in play \nalong with strengthening the already strong area of high pressure \nthat is in place over the eastern half of the country. Forecast \nsoundings remain dry through the period amid subsidence. Thus mostly \nsunny days and mostly clear nights will be expected. Weak warm air \nadvection is in place on Monday and Tuesday as the core of cold air \nwe have been dealing with moves farther east. Thus highs in the \nlower 50s will be expected on Monday with middle 50s on Tuesday.\n\nWednesday through Sunday...\n\nStrong surface high pressure will slowly continue to settle into the \nregion through the week. Deep subsidence and increasing heights from \nthe subsequent surface high will keep skies mostly clear through \nSaturday. By Wednesday, models are hinting at a subtle shortwave \ntrough forming over the Great Plains in the wake of the upper level \nridge. A subsequent surface low pressure system is expected to form \nover the Northern Plains with a cold front extending south towards \nthe Southern Plains/MS River Valley. 15-20 kts of southerly WAA \nahead of the cold front coupled with sufficient boundary layer \nmixing during the day will promote a slow warming trend this week \nwith highs mid week in the low 60s rising to near record high \ntemperatures in the low 70s by the end of the week. Low temperatures \nwill be less mild ranging from the upper 30s midweek to the upper \n40s by the end of the week.\n\nAny chance for rain through the forecast period will be confined to \nSaturday night into Sunday with the passage of the cold front. \nUncertainties in timing/track of the surface low and whether enough \nmoisture will be advected for measurable precipitation reduce \nconfidence in this scenario.",
